<TopicPage slug="subjects/environmental-science/intermediate/air-quality-and-aerosols">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Air pollution is the <strong>world\'s largest environmental killer and its most fixable
    problem</strong>: the physics is known, the sources are measurable, and every serious
    clean-up has worked. It also holds climate science\'s trickiest subtlety — the particles
    that kill us also cool us.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="PM2.5, the aerosol paradox, sources, the documented wins" widget="equationStep" config={airCfg} />

  <h2>Why it stays on the agenda</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Household air pollution:</strong> ~2.3 billion people cook with solid fuels — indoor smoke is a top killer in low-income countries. Clean-cooking programmes are the highest health-return intervention available (the development lesson\'s RCT territory).</li>
    <li><strong>Wildfire smoke changes the map:</strong> smoke events now deliver hazardous air to cities far from fires (Canada 2023 reached New York) — a new regime that policy is scrambling to meet.</li>
    <li><strong>The equity gradient is steep:</strong> monitors and studies show pollution tracks income and race (the sociology lesson\'s environmental justice data) — the burden and the benefit of clean-up are unevenly shared by design of where things were sited.</li>
    <li><strong>The trade-off made explicit:</strong> shipping-fuel sulfur rules (2020) cut 150,000 deaths/yr projected — and slightly unmasked warming. Choosing clean air and paying the climate bill honestly (mitigation) is the correct framing; choosing smog to stay cool is not.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> WHO Global Air Quality Guidelines (2021); the Clean Air Acts; Beijing action-plan evaluations (UNEP, 2020); the atmosphere lesson for meteorology.</p>
</TopicPage>
